  CTX-0070 [architecture] MAY_BE_STALE (test/ticket-intake.test.js changed) — Source intake: - Material user artifacts are registered as immutable sources under .yallaflow/sources/SRC-*/: the original bytes are copied unchanged, and source.json records format, metadata and links. - YallaFlow's own text representations (native-text rawText in source.json, extracted.txt) are written with recognizable secret values redacted, and intake warns when a captured original appears to contain secrets. - Formats: office and PDF are extracted, images get no OCR, and archives are never unpacked. - Format is detected from content, and writes are confined to the source directory. - Tracker tickets (intake ticket, v1.1.0) are ordinary v2 file sources (detectedFormat 'ticket', origin in metadata.ticket, previousSource on re-import): the provider payload is the original, the rendered untrusted-content-bannered Markdown with [description]/[ac:N]/[comment:id] locators is the extracted text, and each attachment is its own source.
  CTX-0073 [architecture] MAY_BE_STALE (test/quality-qc.test.js changed) — Verification evidence: 'yallaflow verify' runs argv commands by default (spawnSync with shell:false); --shell and --script are explicit alternatives. Each run is appended to work/<id>/evidence/verification.json with its full stdout/stderr log file, capturing up to 64 MiB of output (v1.1.0; Node's 1 MiB default had recorded verbose suites as failed). Recognizable secret values are redacted in the log and in the recorded command; the executed command is unchanged. Failed runs are kept. Optional QC and demo records never write this ledger.
  CTX-0075 [integration] MAY_BE_STALE (test/ticket-intake.test.js changed) — Network access exists only in the explicit, opt-in 'yallaflow intake ticket <ref> --fetch' (v1.1.0): src/intake/providers/http.js issues read-only GET requests with a timeout and size cap. Credentials are read from environment variables and sent only over https to the hosted tracker (api.github.com, *.atlassian.net, dev.azure.com, *.visualstudio.com) or to an origin the project configured explicitly in requirements.providers (the only way to trust GitHub Enterprise, Jira Data Center, Azure DevOps Server, or http); a ticket URL alone never decides where a token goes, and the check runs before any request. Credentialed requests never follow cross-origin redirects; attachments download only from the tracker origin or GitHub's own attachment hosts; credentials are never persisted or printed. The provider layer is lazily imported only by the intake command. Every other command makes no network call, no model/provider call, and no telemetry. The only other external tool is local read-only Git; YallaFlow never commits, pushes or rewrites branches.
